## Claude Free: what you actually get

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/claude-free.png" name="Claude Free" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** The $0 tier of Claude on web, desktop, iOS and Android. Anthropic's pricing page calls it "Free for everyone" and the help center says anyone in a supported location can sign up with an email address. Anthropic's pricing page lists no card requirement or trial period for Free.

**Who it is for.** Occasional users, students who only need a tutor now and then, anyone testing whether Claude fits their work before paying, and people who already pay for another assistant and want a second opinion.

**What is included.** From the pricing page's comparison table, as of November 25, 2026:

- Chat on web, desktop and mobile
- Web search, file creation and code execution
- Memory across conversations (the pricing page lists Memory as Yes for Free)
- Connectors and desktop extensions (the table says Yes for Free)
- Artifacts, plus Claude Design, Slides and Docs
- Skills (Yes on Free in the pricing table)
- Voice mode, incognito chats, user preferences
- Up to 5 projects
- Sonnet and Haiku models, with a context window up to 1M tokens depending on the model

**What is not included.** Opus, Fable, Claude Code, Research, hand-off and scheduled tasks, Claude Data, Sheets and Whiteboards (beta), Claude in Chrome, Claude for Microsoft 365 and Outlook, Claude Science, usage credits, and priority access at busy times.

**Pros**

- The widest free feature list among the Claude plans, now including Docs, Slides and Design. Anthropic took those out of beta on October 8, 2026 and put them on every plan including Free, which the @claudeai account announced the same day.
- Sign-up needs an email address, and the pricing page lists no trial expiry. It works on web, desktop and mobile.
- You can use skills and connectors, so Free is a real way to test agent-style workflows.

**Cons**

- Anthropic publishes no message count. The pricing page says "there's no fixed message count" and Free "covers everyday questions".
- No Opus and no Claude Code. If you came for coding, Free will disappoint.
- Limits can change at Anthropic's discretion, and Anthropic says it does not guarantee uninterrupted service.

**Benefits for your use case.** Free is enough to draft documents, summarize PDFs, make a slide deck, write and run a small script, and keep a lightweight memory of who you are. It is not enough for all-day work.

**Pricing and limits.** $0. The pricing page says "Limited usage every 5 hours and every week". The Get started article says the free plan has "a session-based usage limit that will reset every five hours" and that message counts vary with demand. These two pages differ slightly: the pricing page also mentions a weekly limit for Free, while the help article describes only the five-hour session. We cite both rather than pick one.

**Standout use case.** Turn a messy brief into a Docs, Slides or Design artifact without paying. Anthropic's October 8 post says you and your team can edit the same doc, deck or design together with Claude.

{% x id="2108271559928389679" /%}

**Skip it if** you need Claude Code, long research runs, Opus-level reasoning, or you routinely hit a limit. Go to Pro.

**Setup.** Go to claude.ai, sign in with an email address, and open Settings to check Usage. Anthropic says you can see where you stand at Settings > Usage.

## Claude Pro: the plan most people end up on

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/claude-pro.png" name="Claude Pro" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Anthropic's individual paid plan. The pricing page shows $17 per month with an annual subscription discount ($200 billed up front) or $20 if billed monthly. Anthropic's Pro help article gives $20 per month for the US and says regional prices and tax treatment vary. Prices on the pricing page exclude tax.

**Who it is for.** People who use Claude most days, developers who want Claude Code, and anyone who keeps seeing a limit message on Free.

**Key additions over Free.** More usage (the pricing page says Pro gives "at least 5x more usage per 5-hour session than Free"), Opus, Claude Code, Research, hand-off and scheduled tasks, Claude Data, Sheets and Whiteboards in beta, Claude Science, Claude in Chrome, Claude for Microsoft 365 and Outlook, Projects without the five-project Free cap, usage credits (so work can continue past the plan's included usage), and Fable paid with usage credits.

Anthropic's October 8 post says Claude Dashboards, which build live dashboards from a connected data platform or CRM, are in beta on paid plans:

{% x id="2108271555822092483" /%}

**Pros**

- The jump from Free is large for about $17 to $20 a month.
- Weekly and five-hour limits that you can inspect in Settings > Usage, and usage credits so work continues instead of stopping.
- Claude Code is included in all paid plans and shares the same pool as chat. Our sister site covers that tool separately in its [free Claude Code guide](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/free-claude-code-guide).

**Cons**

- No standing free trial and no student discount (details below).
- Chat and Claude Code draw from one usage pool, so a long coding session can drain the same allowance you use for chat.
- Pro does not include API usage. Anthropic's help center says you pay for the Claude Console API separately.

**Pricing and limits.** $17/mo annual or $20/mo monthly. Limits depend on message length, model, features and effort level, so there is no fixed number.

**Standout use case.** Daily writing plus Claude Code in the terminal on one subscription.

**Skip it if** you rarely hit Free's limits or you only need ChatGPT-style Q and A. Also skip the annual plan if you only want to pay for term time: $20 for nine months is $180, versus $200 for a year (simple arithmetic on the posted prices).

**Setup.** Upgrade from claude.ai or claude.com/pricing and pick monthly or annual billing. Anthropic says that if you upgrade after hitting a limit, your new plan's limits apply right away.

## Claude Max (and Ultra): only if Pro keeps stopping you

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/claude-max.png" name="Claude Max" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** The heavy-use tier. Pricing page, November 25, 2026: Max is $100 per month and gives 5x Pro usage every 5 hours and every week, with Fable included up to about half your weekly limit and $100 a month in API credits. Ultra is $200 per month with 10x Pro weekly usage, no five-hour limit for verified accounts, and $200 a month in API credits. Both are billed monthly, according to the pricing page's billing-cycle row. Both include priority access at high traffic times.

**Who it is for.** Developers who run Claude Code for hours, researchers with long documents, consultants and agencies working in Claude all day.

**Pros**

- Priority access when Claude is busy.
- Included monthly API credits, which is unusual for a chat subscription.
- Ultra removes the five-hour wall for verified accounts.

**Cons**

- A $100 to $200 monthly commitment, billed monthly; the pricing page lists no annual option for Max or Ultra.
- Anthropic still says it may impose other limits at its discretion.

**Skip it if** you have not first tried Pro and measured how often you hit the limit. That is our reading of Anthropic's own ladder (Pro, then Max at 5x, then Ultra), not a tested recommendation.

## Claude for Education: free only if your university pays

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/claude-for-education.png" name="Claude for Education" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** A university-wide plan. Anthropic's pricing page describes the Education plan as "a comprehensive university-wide plan for an institution, including its students, faculty, and staff". Anthropic launched Claude for Education on April 2, 2025, with campus-wide agreements at Northeastern University, the London School of Economics and Political Science, and Champlain College, plus a learning mode that guides reasoning rather than handing over answers.

**What a student gets.** Anthropic's help article for students says a university-sponsored account includes an enhanced context window, Projects, increased usage limits ("more messages per day compared to individual plans") and priority access during high-traffic periods. It also says your university may customize features, so two schools' accounts may differ. The sign-in route is claude.ai with your university email.

**Pros**

- Higher limits and Projects without paying yourself.
- The school holds the contract, so what you pay (if anything) depends on the school.

**Cons**

- It only exists if your university has signed. A .edu address on a plain free account unlocks nothing by itself.
- Anthropic publishes no school directory. Even at schools that license Claude, access may not extend to students: the University of Iowa announced Claude for Education on November 17, 2026 for faculty and staff with paid licenses ($20 per user per month standard, $55 premium), and said individual student licenses are not supported (that is the school's own page, not Anthropic's).

**Is there a student discount?** No standard one. Anthropic's Pro help article says: "We don't offer standard discounted pricing for individual plans (Pro, Max, and Ultra)", that it occasionally runs limited-time promotions, and that it has no standing discounts available upon request. Be wary of third-party sites selling a student Pro code. The April 2025 launch also announced a Campus Ambassadors program and a way for student builders to apply for API credits, neither of which is a price cut on Pro.

**Learning mode.** Anthropic introduced learning mode with Claude for Education in April 2025 as a way to guide a student's reasoning rather than hand over answers. Its higher-education page says it works "like a good tutor: it asks questions that help you find the answers yourself". We could not confirm from Anthropic's pages whether learning mode is available on the Free plan, so check inside the app.

{% figure src="https://assets.claude.com/16ed2cfc21c6e91fb38b6b8ea4b66045f82e12e9.jpg?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop" alt="Claude for higher education page social card" caption="Claude for higher education, Anthropic's page for university plans and learning mode. Source: Anthropic (claude.com/solutions/education)" /%}

**Skip it if** your university has no agreement and you only need study help. Use Free, and ask your IT desk whether a deal exists.

**Setup.** Open claude.ai, choose sign in with your university email, and follow your school's sign-in steps (some schools use single sign-on). If nothing appears, ask IT. Anthropic's help page says to check with your university's IT department for institution-specific guidelines.

## Claude for Teachers: free for verified US K-12 educators

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/claude-for-teachers.png" name="Claude for Teachers" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Anthropic introduced Claude for Teachers on July 14, 2026: free access to premium Claude capabilities for verified K-12 educators in the US, a library of teaching skills, and a connection to evidence-based curricula mapped to academic standards in all 50 states. Anthropic's post was updated on November 13, 2026 to say the free program now runs through July 30, 2028.

{% x id="2077047278078931243" /%}

**What is included.** Per Anthropic's announcement: the Learning Commons connector, tailored teaching skills co-developed with Learning Commons, Claude Code and Cowork (so Claude can run scheduled tasks), connectors to K-12 tools such as ASSISTments, Brisk Teaching, Canva Education, Diffit, Eedi, MagicSchool, Snorkl and TeachFX, and two AI fluency courses (one co-created with Teach For America, plus a train-the-trainer module co-created with the American Federation of Teachers). Training on teacher conversations is off for verified accounts.

**Who it is for.** US K-12 teachers. The announcement says it is for educators only, consistent with Claude's 18-and-over policy. Anthropic launched a separate school and district offering on August 28, 2026.

**Pros**

- A Claude route that gives teachers Claude Code and Cowork at $0, which the Free plan does not include.
- Standards-aligned lesson planning through the Learning Commons connector, per Anthropic.
- K-12 privacy terms, including a K-12 data processing addendum with FERPA-aligned protections.

**Cons**

- US only, and teachers only. Teachers outside the US, and university lecturers, are not covered by this program. Higher-education faculty should look at Claude for Education.
- The program page notes that extra usage limits apply.
- Anthropic's own August 28 update says using identifiable student data requires authorization from the school or district, so keep examples de-identified.

**Skip it if** you teach outside the US or at a university. Free or your school's plan is the route.

**Setup.** Start at [claude.com/solutions/teachers](https://claude.com/solutions/teachers) and complete the verification step.

{% figure src="https://assets.claude.com/9a63e9fd69f540988eb79d5e9db8cc2a6884a7f5.jpg?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop" alt="Claude for K-12 Teachers page social card" caption="Claude for K-12 Teachers, Anthropic's program page. Source: Anthropic (claude.com/solutions/teachers)" /%}

## Claude Team plan for scientists: a free seat for lab groups

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/claude-team-plan-for-scientists.png" name="Claude Team plan for scientists" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Anthropic's help center describes it as discounted access to a Claude Team plan for academic and non-profit research groups. Standard seats are $0 per user per month and Premium seats (five times more usage) are $15 per user per month, or $180 per year. The pricing applies for 12 months from sign-up, is offered to a limited number of groups, and seats renew at the then-current price afterward, with admins notified first. The page says workspaces already on the discounted plan moved to this pricing for charges after August 27, 2026.

**Who it is for.** Principal investigators (or equivalents) at accredited universities and nonprofit research institutes in natural sciences, mathematics, computer science, engineering and related fields. Once verified, a PI can add postdocs, graduate students and researchers, from 1 to 25 seats per group. A grad student cannot apply alone. The request goes through the PI.

**Included.** Claude Science, Claude Code and Claude Cowork, central billing, single sign-on, and monthly API credits ($20 per Standard seat and $100 per Premium seat, pooled and capped at $500 a month, per the help center).

**Cons and fine print.** Not for for-profit companies. If your campus already has an enterprise agreement, check with your institution first. Groups with 90 days of no activity can be moved back to standard pricing. Most applications are reviewed within seven business days. After 12 months seats renew at the then-current price, so set a calendar reminder.

**Skip it if** you are a student in the humanities, law or business with no PI. There is no equivalent lab route for you.

{% figure src="https://assets.claude.com/ad648331984e9620b2418d2ac9ef752a5e59caa4.jpg?w=1200&h=630&fit=crop" alt="Claude Team plan for scientists program page social card" caption="The Claude Team plan for scientists program page. Source: Anthropic (claude.com/programs/team-plan-for-scientists)" /%}

## ChatGPT Free: the main rival

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/chatgpt-free.png" name="ChatGPT Free" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** OpenAI's $0 tier. OpenAI's Free Tier FAQ says Free users have access to GPT-5.6 Luna, and that Think is available in the mobile app and rolling out on the web. Free can search the web, analyze data, upload images or files, use existing GPTs, create images, and use a Library with 512 MB of storage.

**Limits.** OpenAI says "Free users have unlimited everyday text chats, subject to abuse-prevention safeguards". File uploads, image generation, voice, data analysis and other tools have separate usage limits. Ads may appear in the Free tier in certain countries. New GPT creation is not available on personal accounts, including Free.

**Paid ladder.** OpenAI's January 16, 2026 post listed Go at $8 (US, localized in some markets), Plus at $20 and Pro at $200. We did not re-capture chatgpt.com/pricing today, so check it before you rely on those numbers; OpenAI says it may adjust pricing. OpenAI's Go help page says annual billing is not supported for Go, Plus or Pro.

**Pros:** The most generous plain-text chat allowance of the free tiers, and an inexpensive $8 Go step. **Cons:** Ads in some countries, a fragmented limit on tools, and no custom GPT creation. **Skip it if** you need a model that works with skills and connectors the way Claude does, though ChatGPT has its own plugins directory and, per OpenAI's January post, Codex on Plus. Our blog has a cost-planning look at [Codex usage limits](https://agentskills.codes/blog/codex-usage-limits-agent-workflows) if you build on the OpenAI side.

## Gemini free: the Google Account bundle

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/gemini-free.png" name="Gemini free" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Google's free plan: "$0 / month with a Google Account". The subscriptions page lists, for Free, the Gemini app with auto model selection, image generation and editing, Deep Research, Gemini Live and Canvas, Skills, limited access to Google Flow 2 with Nano Banana Pro, Gemini Notebook, and 15 GB of storage, and it says Free includes Gemini 4 Hydrogen.

**Limits.** Google's help page says Gemini Apps have compute-based usage limits that factor in prompt complexity, model, features and chat length, and that "Your limit refreshes every 5 hours until you reach your weekly limit". For people without a Google AI plan, features that need more compute, such as Deep Research, may be unavailable during high demand. Google may change limits without notice.

**Paid ladder.** Google AI Plus gives double Free's usage limits and Google AI Pro four times, per the subscriptions page. We did not capture Plus or Pro prices in our extract, so we leave them out. The page shows Google AI Ultra at $99.99 and $199.99 per month for 5x and 20x Pro limits.

**Pros:** The strongest free multimodal bundle (images, Deep Research, Live voice) and a familiar five-hour plus weekly limit shape. **Cons:** Opaque limits, and high-demand throttling for free users. **Skip it if** your team does not use Google Workspace and you want the Claude file-and-code workflow. For Google's model news, see our [Gemini 4 Argon explainer](https://agentskills.codes/blog/gemini-4-argon-explained).

## Meta Muse free: an agent with a free allowance

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/is-claude-free/logos/meta-muse-free.png" name="Meta Muse free" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Meta's help center describes Muse as "your personal AI agent that can take actions and help get things done with your direction". It says Muse "is available for free with a usage limit" and that if you reach it, you can wait or upgrade.

**Paid ladder.** Per Meta's help page (it showed "Updated: 6 days ago" when we fetched it): Power is $20 per month with 500M Muse tokens per week and 10 phone calls per day, and Maximum is $100 per month with 3B Muse tokens per week and 20 phone calls per day. Meta says Muse and its subscriptions are in limited testing and not available in all locations. Subscribers must be 18 or the age of majority.

**What we could not verify.** Meta does not publish the size of the free allowance in the page we read, and we could not confirm which models the free tier runs. We did not test Muse. For a model-level view of Meta's frontier model, see our [Muse Wonder scorecard](https://agentskills.codes/blog/muse-wonder-meta-scorecard-agent-builders).

**Pros:** Free to start, an action-taking agent, and a clearly priced $20 and $100 ladder. **Cons:** Limited testing, location gating, and an unpublished free allowance. **Skip it if** you need a documented limit or you are outside a supported country.

## How Claude's limits work (and how to stretch Free)

Anthropic describes two kinds of limits. Usage limits cap how much you can do over a period. Length limits cap how long a single conversation can get. They are different problems with different fixes.

- **Five-hour limit.** Anthropic's help center says the period starts when you send your first message, and your limit resets in full five hours later. The Get started article describes the free plan's limit the same way: session-based, resetting every five hours.
- **Weekly limits.** Anthropic says weekly limits apply across all models and reset at a fixed time each week assigned to your account. The pricing page lists a weekly limit for Free too.
- **One shared pool.** Claude on the web, Claude Code and Claude Desktop all count toward the same usage limit.
- **No fixed number.** What you can do depends on message length, attached file length, conversation length, and the model or feature you use.
- **Long chats cost more.** With code execution on, Claude automatically summarizes earlier messages in a long conversation. Anthropic says longer conversations that trigger this consume more of your usage limit.

Practical ways to stretch Free, drawn from Anthropic's own context and limits guidance rather than our testing:

1. Start a new conversation when you approach your limit in a long chat.
2. Put reference material in a project. Anthropic says projects use retrieval, so only relevant content loads into the context window.
3. Keep project instructions short and remove files you no longer use.
4. Lower the effort level, or turn thinking off where the model has a toggle, for routine tasks.
5. Turn off connectors and web search when a conversation does not need them. Anthropic says tools and connectors are token-intensive.
6. Check Settings > Usage (the "Current session" bar) before a big task, not after.

When you hit a limit, Anthropic says you can wait for it to reset or upgrade your plan, and your new plan's limits apply right away. On paid plans you can also enable usage credits so work continues instead of stopping.

